# Cross-repo siblings cloned by setup-dev-env at workspace-lock-pinned
# revisions. They are io-mon's `uses:` library producers in repro.nim
# (reprobuild builds each from source and threads its `src/` root onto
# io-mon's compile); they are also the `--path` siblings config.nims
# resolves for the plain `nim c` / `nimble` build.
#
# nim-stackable-hooks carries an ACKNOWLEDGED override ('!=') because the
# workspace-lock pin is stale and cannot un-stale itself. The lock record
# `clone-siblings` resolves for a mainline io-mon commit is produced by
# `publish-workspace-lock.yml`, which is a FORWARD CARRY: it copies the
# previous tip's sibling set verbatim onto the new HEAD and, in its own words,
# "if a pushed commit BUMPED a sibling, the carried set predates that bump".
# Nothing in io-mon's normal merge flow ever re-resolves that set, so the
# carried pin froze at nim-stackable-hooks 25bf49e4 ("windows: stop freeing the
# remote path buffer under a live LoadLibraryW") and every io-mon commit since
# has inherited it.
#
# That revision predates 6a53408 ("Windows injector: an explicit child
# environment"), which is where `src/stackable_hooks/windows_env_block.nim` and
# `runWithMonitorShim`'s `env` parameter arrived. So
# `tests/portable/test_io_mon_windows_child_env_block.nim` — which imports
# `stackable_hooks/windows_env_block` and is portable on purpose, see its
# header — could not compile in CI at all:
#
#   tests/portable/test_io_mon_windows_child_env_block.nim(41, 23)
#     Error: cannot open file: stackable_hooks/windows_env_block
#
# and `nimble test` aborts at the first failing file, so the whole host-OS
# suite behind it never ran either. It passes on a developer machine because
# the workspace sibling checkout there is a live `dev` tree that has the file;
# the stale pin is only consulted in CI.
#
# 72f57824 is the revision `flake.nix` pins `stackable-hooks-src` to, so this
# entry names the SAME commit as the flake input rather than introducing a
# third opinion. Keep the two in step: a `nim c` in the dev shell resolves
# `stackable_hooks/...` through this checkout, while `nix build .#default`
# resolves it through the flake input, and a build that disagrees about which
# nim-stackable-hooks it is compiling against is the failure this file exists
# to prevent.
#
# Drop the '!=' back to a bare name once a re-locked mainline commit pins
# 72f57824 or later; until then a bare name silently reinstates 25bf49e4.
nim-stackable-hooks!=72f578249e9d8bbca8e3705c8a41ed5085c05bf9
nim-shm-queue

# nim-shm-gset is required by `src/io_mon/writer.nim` (`import
# shm_gset/transport`) and by scripts/build_shim.sh, but it was missing from
# this list, so `just build` failed with "missing nim-shm-gset at
# ../nim-shm-gset/src" and `just test` with "cannot open file:
# shm_gset/transport". Both were reported as PASSING jobs, because those
# steps pipe into `tee` and the default `run` shell has no pipefail; ci.yml
# now sets `shell: bash` so the exit status survives.
#
# Pinned to an explicit revision rather than resolved from the workspace
# lock: the io-mon locks published to metacraft-manifests (8b6d0b9b,
# ea5fa77a) list only io-mon, nim-stackable-hooks and nim-shm-queue, so a
# bare `nim-shm-gset` entry would fail resolution instead of cloning. The
# durable fix is to add nim-shm-gset to the io-mon workspace manifest so it
# is locked alongside its siblings; this pin should be dropped then.
# cf0adf2f is metacraft-labs/nim-shm-gset@dev, the revision io-mon's build
# and full test suite are green against.
#
# Spelled '!=' rather than '=': the lock now DOES pin nim-shm-gset (at
# 8a9f2f13), so a bare '=' entry is an unacknowledged override and
# `clone-siblings` says so on every run. The revision is unchanged — only the
# acknowledgement is added — which is what lets this repo eventually set
# `on-lock-override: error` and have the stale-pin class above fail the job
# instead of merely warning.
nim-shm-gset!=cf0adf2f39647f5b203b2ab694414a3c50e93951
